From 30bb753e6149e812f1c4c6da6b936471cc3428de Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Z User <z@container>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 16:47:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] security(ipc): authenticate Cowork daemon clients with a
 capability token (S-1,S-2,S-7,S-10)

The named-pipe / WebSocket daemon served every method - including
approve_tool_call - to any same-user local process, and broadcast pending
approval IDs to every connected client, letting a rogue process drive the
agent and self-approve destructive actions.

- Daemon now generates a 256-bit capability token at startup and writes it
  to a 0600 owner-only file in the user temp dir BEFORE the pipe accepts
  its first client. The desktop GUI reads the token and sends
  auth: { token } with every request; RpcConnection::open fails fast when
  the token is missing.
- Every JSON-RPC method (including ping and notifications) requires the
  token, verified with a constant-time comparison; failures return
  -32001 before any handler runs.
- CoworkEvent stream is only forwarded to connections that have
  authenticated at least once, so approval IDs are never disclosed to
  unauthenticated listeners.
- S-7: named-pipe lines are read with a bounded reader (1 MiB cap,
  incremental fill - oversized lines fail fast instead of buffering) and
  the WebSocket fallback caps message/frame sizes at the same limit.
- S-10: pipe instances now set reject_remote_clients(true).
- New tests: unauthorized rejection (missing + wrong token, no task
  started), bounded reader oversized/partial-EOF behavior, token
  uniqueness/shape; existing RPC tests updated for the auth field.

Residual risks documented in the finalization report: a process running
as the same user that can read the token file is trusted (Windows pipe
ACLs would tighten this further); the GUI crate cannot be compiled in the
audit sandbox (webkit2gtk/Windows-only) and is review-verified.
